<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Dandanaqan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1040">1040</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Dandanaqan was fought in 1040 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Turks</a> and the Ghaznavid army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Mas'ud. The Seljuks won a decisive victory. It gave them control of Khurasan and marked the foundation of the Seljuk Empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-dandanaqan.jpg" alt="Battle of Dandanaqan" width="300" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gaznaly-seljuk_Dandanaqan.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> were forced out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> in 1034 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanids</a> and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> under the protection of the Ghaznavid governor Harun. After his murder in 1035 they fled across the Kara Kum Desert towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>, but turned instead to Nasa on the edge of Khurasan. The Ghaznavid sultan Ma'sud I sent Iltughdi with a large army to Nasa, and it at first drove off the Seljuk forces. Then it began squabbling over the spoils. The Seljuks, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaghri-beg">Chaghri</a>, returned, fell on the disorganised Ghaznavids and defeated them. Ma'sud then granted the Seljuks three cities in Khurasan, Dihistan, Nasa and Farawa, and after raids as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a> all of Khurasan fell to them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>During Ma'sud's march to Sarakhs, Seljuk raiders harassed his army with hit-and-run tactics. Swift and mobile, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmens</a> were better suited to fighting in steppe and desert than the heavily laden and conservative Ghaznavid army. They also destroyed its supply lines and cut it off from the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>, which seriously reduced its discipline and morale. On 23 May 1040 around 16,000 Seljuk soldiers fought a starving and demoralised Ghaznavid army at Dandanaqan, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merv">Merv</a>, and destroyed a large part of it. Ma'sud fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, was overthrown and was finally murdered in prison.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The victory brought down Ghaznavid domination in Greater Khorasan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seljuk-empire">Seljuks</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistan">Sistan</a>, Pushang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghor">Ghur</a>, put Musa Yabghu bin Seljuk in charge, and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkh">Balkh</a>. By 1047 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughril">Tughril</a> had coins minted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> that called him al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> al-Mu'azzam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahanshah">Shahanshah</a>. The Seljuks lost Khurasan to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazmian-empire">Khwarazmian Empire</a> in 1138, which in turn lost it to the Ghoris in 1173.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Dandanaqan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army's weakness appeared under his son Mas'ud I, whose heavy columns, slowed by baggage and elephants, could not pin down the mobile Seljuk Turkmen horsemen, who finally defeated them at the <b>Battle of Dandanaqan</b> in 1040.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1040, as the Seljuks defeated the Ghaznavids at the <b>Battle of Dandanaqan</b>, Ibrahim ibn Nasr, known as Böritegin and later as Tamghach Khan, took control of Bukhara and Samarqand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kara-khanid-khanate">Kara-Khanid Khanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>At the <b>Battle of Dandanaqan</b> in 1040 Tughril and Chaghri routed the army of the Ghaznavid sultan Masud I. The victory secured Khorasan and opened the Iranian plateau to Seljuk expansion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seljuk-empire">Seljuk Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
